#!/bin/sh
# <manwriter>
# ezbacksnap - a Timeshift replacement for btrfs: system snapshots on a schedule, a snapshot before every
# update, roll back or restore single files, plus rotating rsync backups of directories.
#
#   ezbacksnap                          menu (snapshots, restore, schedule, update hook)
#   ezbacksnap snapshot [create [LABEL]|list|prune|delete NAME]
#   ezbacksnap snapshot restore NAME [--with-home] [--yes]    roll the system back (reboot afterwards)
#   ezbacksnap snapshot get NAME PATH [DEST]                  one file or folder out of a snapshot
#   ezbacksnap snapshot cleanup [--yes]                       delete the old subvolumes a rollback left
#   ezbacksnap timer install [hourly daily weekly monthly boot] | remove | status
#   ezbacksnap hook install|remove|status                     a snapshot before pacman / apt transactions
#   ezbacksnap backup SRC DEST ...      rsync backups of a directory (advanced, below)
#
# Both keep only the newest N (BACKUP_KEEP and SNAPSHOT_KEEP, the numbers
# at the top of the script; each also an environment override), and both
# pick a backend from the filesystem they work on, so they behave the same
# on btrfs and on everything else.
#
# BACKUP copies the contents of SRC into DEST/YYYY-MM-DD_HHMMSS, then
# deletes the oldest ones beyond BACKUP_KEEP. It is as complete as SNAPSHOT:
#   backup list DEST                the copies, oldest first, with sizes
#   backup prune DEST [KEEP]        drop the oldest beyond KEEP
#   backup delete DEST NAME         drop one
#   backup restore DEST NAME TARGET [--dry-run] [--delete]
#                                   copy a backup back; only changed files are written and
#                                   nothing is removed from TARGET unless --delete is given
#   backup add JOB SRC DEST [KEEP] | remove JOB | jobs | run JOB|--all
#                                   named jobs kept in ~/.config/ezbacksnap/jobs (EZBACKSNAP_JOBS),
#                                   e.g. `ezbacksnap backup run --all` from cron or a timer
# The menu has all of it, next to the snapshot entries.
#   btrfs   rsync mirrors SRC into the subvolume DEST/current, then a
#           read-only snapshot of it is taken (copy-on-write, unchanged
#           data costs nothing). Old ones go with "btrfs subvolume delete"
#           (via sudo if not root).
#   others  (ext4, xfs, ...) rsync --link-dest against the previous copy:
#           unchanged files are hard links, so every copy looks complete
#           but only changes take space.
# A run that fails leaves the existing copies alone and never counts.
#
# SNAPSHOT snapshots whole mount points (TARGETS, / and /home by default)
# as one named set, YYYY-MM-DD_HHMMSS plus an optional _LABEL. It runs
# itself through sudo. Backend from the filesystem of the first target
# (SNAPSHOT_BACKEND forces one):
#   btrfs  read-only copy-on-write snapshots. The top-level subvolume is
#          mounted just for the run and the snapshots go to SNAP_SUBVOL on
#          it (created if missing), so they need no mount of their own.
#          btrfs refuses to snapshot a subvolume that holds an active
#          swapfile; that target is reported and the others are still done.
#   zfs    "zfs snapshot -r DATASET@NAME" on each target's dataset.
#   rsync  any other filesystem: a hard-linked full copy under RSYNC_DIR.
# TIERS. A label after the timestamp is the tier: `snapshot create daily` makes DATE_TIME_daily. Each label is
# rotated on its own, by SNAPSHOT_TIERS (hourly:6 daily:5 weekly:3 monthly:2 boot:5 pre-update:5, the Timeshift
# defaults); manual snapshots and any other label use SNAPSHOT_KEEP. `timer install` runs them from systemd
# timers (cron if there is no systemd), `hook install` adds the pre-update one for pacman or apt. Both put a copy
# of this script in /usr/local/bin first, so moving this folder never breaks the schedule.
#
# ROLLBACK (btrfs): `snapshot restore NAME` does what Timeshift does. For / (and /home only with --with-home):
# the current state is snapshotted as DATE_before-restore, the live subvolume is renamed NAME.replaced-DATE
# (the running system keeps using it until the reboot) and a writable copy of the snapshot becomes the new NAME.
# Nothing is copied or deleted; a failure undoes itself. It refuses when the bootloader selects the root by
# subvolume id instead of name (rootflags=subvol=@). `snapshot cleanup` deletes the .replaced-* leftovers once
# the restored system works. `snapshot get NAME PATH [DEST]` takes a single file or folder back instead (a
# file that is replaced is kept next to it as .before-restore-DATE).
#
# Booting a snapshot from the boot menu is grub-btrfs's job (it lists @snapshots); not part of this tool.
#
# Verification note: the btrfs and rsync backends were run; the zfs one
# was written against zfs's documented commands and never run.
#
# @flag backup SRC DEST:rotating rsync backup of a directory
# @flag backup list|prune|delete|restore ...:manage and restore the copies of a destination
# @flag backup add|remove|jobs|run ...:named backup jobs
# @flag snapshot [create [LABEL]]:take a system snapshot now
# @flag snapshot list:list snapshots with their tier
# @flag snapshot restore NAME [--with-home]:roll the system back to a snapshot
# @flag snapshot get NAME PATH [DEST]:one file or folder from a snapshot
# @flag snapshot cleanup:delete the old subvolumes after a rollback
# @flag timer install|remove|status:hourly/daily/weekly/monthly/boot snapshots on a schedule
# @flag hook install|remove|status:a snapshot before every pacman or apt transaction
# @flag snapshot prune:delete everything beyond the newest SNAPSHOT_KEEP
# @flag snapshot delete NAME:delete one snapshot
# </manwriter>

